<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Kongo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kongo Empire, Kongo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of Kongo was a Central African state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Mbanza Kongo</a> in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, flourishing from the fourteenth century. It entered into contact with Portugal in the 1480s, and its rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Its history, covered in the Kongo Empire profile, was shaped by trade, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and conflict with the Portuguese.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kongo" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oren neu dag · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Kongo_according_to_Giovanni_Cavazzi_da_Montecuccolo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Oral traditions of Kongo's early history were first written down in the late 16th century, with detailed versions recorded in the mid-17th century by, among others, the Italian Capuchin missionary Giovanni Cavazzi da Montecuccolo. The traditions about its foundation changed over time with circumstances. Jean Cuvelier published a KiKongo summary in 1934, but the traditions more probably relate to clan traditions and especially to the period after 1750.</p><p>By the 13th century there were three main federations of states in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo Basin</a>. The Seven Kingdoms of Kongo dia Nlaza lay in the east and were considered the oldest and most powerful. To their south lay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpemba">Mpemba</a>, and west of them across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> a confederation of three small states, Vungu, Kakongo and Ngoyo. A 16th-century tradition said the former kingdoms were now subjects and tributaries of the king of Congo. As centralisation grew, allied provinces lost influence, and by 1620 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbata">Mbata</a>, once a co-kingdom, was known only by the title Grandfather of the King of Kongo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After becoming king in 1509, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I</a> worked to create a viable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in Kongo, financed from royal assets and taxation. With advisers from Portugal, among them the royal chaplain Rui d'Aguiar, he created a syncretic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> that remained part of Kongo's culture for the rest of its independent existence. Álvaro I's seizure of the throne during the crisis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaga">Jaga</a> invasion of 1568 began a new royal line, the House of Kwilu.</p><p>Relations with Portugal worsened as the governors of Portuguese Angola grew more aggressive. Luis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a> de Vasconcelos, who arrived in 1617, used mercenary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbangala">Imbangala</a> to raid southern Kongo provinces, and his successor João Correia de Sousa launched a full-scale invasion of southern Kongo in 1622. From 1636 the House of Kinlaza ruled. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila">Battle of Mbwila</a>, the rival Kimpanzu and Kinlaza factions fought a civil war of about 40 years, which increased the number of Kongo people sold across the Atlantic, weakened the monarchy and strengthened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The village, called vata, was the basic social unit after the family, headed by a chief called nkuluntu. Villages were grouped into small states led by a mwene. The king, the Mwene Kongo, served for life and was chosen by senior officials, and the succession was one of the central problems of Kongo history. Its universal currency was the nzimbu shell, with 2,000 nzimbu buying a goat. The army was a mass levy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> with a smaller corps of shield-bearing heavy infantry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Kongo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Loango was created by <b>Kongo</b> people on the Atlantic coast north of the more powerful Kingdom of Kongo, in what is now the western Republic of the Congo, southern Gabon and Cabinda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Those collected by the Jesuit Baltasar Barreira in the late sixteenth century named the founder as Ngola Kiluanje, also called Ngola Inene, a migrant from <b>Kongo</b>, while another tradition told of an ironworker, Musuri, who rose to be king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their religious practices became foundational to Candomblé in Brazil and Santería (Lucumí) in Cuba, while Yoruba, or Nago, elements also entered Haitian Vodou alongside its predominantly Fon and <b>Kongo</b> roots.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese, interested above all in the slave trade, kept a peaceful and profitable relationship with the <b>Kongo</b>'s rulers and nobles; kings such as João I and Afonso I studied Christianity, learned Portuguese and shared the profits of the trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Among them were <b>Kongo</b>, Fon and Yoruba people, whose religions gave Vodou its main structure.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kongo</b> ranked its people by royal descent, by office and by freedom.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kongo">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Kongo</a></li></ul></section>
